Ray,

It's great to see that you're working on this.  Unfortunately (or
fortunately?), I've been working on the same project on and off.
Perhaps we can combine efforts?  We may of course have had different
approaches on different issues.

I did a search through the entire NH codebase looking for what messages
should have a sound with them voice, nonvoice, it didn't matter.  I
also listed sounds that monsters should make, including some voice when
applicable, under the assumption that when hooked in, it would be done
so probabilistically (to keep a speaking monster from talking too much
when fighting you).  The net result is a file containing several
thousand instances where sounds should be hooked in.  More recently, I
started (but haven't spent very much time on) a computer-parsable file
that lists probabilities for playing different options on different,
with assumed support  for playing multiple sounds at once (so that you
can combine a smaller number of sound effects to make a larger number)

At the same time, my partner and I began recording and processing
sounds with effects and filters (via sox).  Most of them turned out
quite well, although I'm still just a tiny fraction of the way through
the list.  The biggest challenge will be voices - with just my partner
and I, we only have two voices, both female.  Filters work well on some
things (for example, creating demonic voices), but I wouldn't want to
try and pull off the full range of voices, especially male ones, from
two women as recording artists  ;)

The current size is 15 megs when compressed well as oggs, with 597
individual files.  Some of them are duplicates (Elaine did a lot of
voice - too much in some cases, really, as it makes too many different
creatures sound alike, so I redid some of them) and should be pruned.
Naturally, most of the sounds are sound effects.  The only ones that
aren't original are a few fair use clips for humor value (for example,
black light sound effects are 5 second Pink Floyd clips  ;) ).  Most
sounds are "sound effects" - different types of objects impacting other
types, rusting, wearing, burning, all sorts of animal sounds, ambient
room sounds, etc.  I actually made myself lose my voice a couple times
in the process, twisting my throat to produce strange sounds which I
would then add effects to  ;)  We kept serious creatures serious, and
silly creatures silly (quantum mechanic rambling includes things like
"Well, if we assume a spherical cow..." or references to phlogiston;
the mail demon sounds somewhat like the AOL voice; etc)

I haven't done *anything* with triggers.  If you have been working on
that, that would be completely wonderful.

Should we try to work together on this, and iron out the differences
between our approaches?  We at Vulture's would love to have the full
range of sound effects in the game; the current range is rather weak.

	- Karen

P.S. - My jabberwock 'burble' sounds like roaring through water; I
